Phase 3 (dedicated UX/accessibility pass) - the first formal audit against
the live desktop app, not attempted until now. PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md
§5's remaining Phase 3 gap explicitly named "no formal axe-or-equivalent
audit, no keyboard/zoom/reduced-motion pass beyond basic nav focusability."
This closes the axe-audit half with real findings and real fixes, against
the actual running app in a real browser, not a static code read.

Method: `desktop/` served via its own Vite dev server (`npm run dev`,
already configured as this project's `netrewind-desktop-dev` preview target),
opened in a real browser tab, axe-core 4.10.2 injected via
`https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com` (not vendored - a one-off audit tool, not a
runtime dependency), run against all 8 shell pages plus the first-run
wizard's 6 steps, in both light and dark color schemes (`prefers-color-scheme`
emulated), and in both Arabic (RTL) and English.

## Round 1 - real violations found

`axe.run(document, {resultTypes: ['violations']})` per page/step:

- **8 shell pages, dark theme**: Incidents (7 nodes), Timeline (17 nodes),
  Diagnostics (2 nodes) - all `color-contrast` (serious): severity badges
  (`.sev-info`, `.sev-error`) failed WCAG AA's 4.5:1 minimum for normal-size
  text. Overview/Host/Rules/Evidence/Settings: 0 violations.
- **8 shell pages, light theme**: Timeline (11 nodes), Diagnostics (1 node) -
  same `.sev-info`/`.sev-error` `color-contrast` failure; Incidents happened
  to render 0 error-severity badges in this data set so it did not surface
  there in this theme, but the same CSS rule was equally broken.
- **Wizard, all 6 steps, both themes**: `landmark-one-main` (moderate, 1 node
  each) and `region` (moderate, 1-7 nodes each) - the wizard's root element
  was a plain `<div className="wizard-overlay">`, not contained in or itself
  a `<main>` landmark, unlike the normal 8-page shell (`App.tsx` already
  wraps its content in `<main className="main">` - only the wizard, which
  replaces that content entirely before onboarding completes, was missing it).

Exact measured contrast ratios (via axe's own failure summaries, not
independently recomputed only - cross-checked by hand too):
- Dark `.sev-info`: 4.09:1 (needs 4.5:1) - `#9a978f` on `#34373b`.
- Dark `.sev-error`: 4.46:1 (needs 4.5:1) - `#d4695f` on `#2c211a`.
- Light `.sev-info`: 4.2:1 (needs 4.5:1) - `#6b6862` on `#e2e0db`.
- Light `.sev-error`: 6.06:1 - already passing, not touched.

## A real, separate finding NOT caught by axe: keyboard operability

Read `desktop/src/components/Sidebar.tsx` after noticing its nav items are
plain `<div role="button" tabIndex={0}>` rather than native `<button>`
elements (a deliberate-looking but incomplete choice - axe's rule set does
not flag "role=button div with an incomplete key handler" the way it flags
missing ARIA attributes, so this needed a manual code read, not just a tool
run). Two real gaps:
1. `onKeyDown` only handled `"Enter"`, not `" "` (Space) - the WAI-ARIA APG
   button pattern requires both. A screen-reader or keyboard-only user
   pressing Space on a focused nav item (very common muscle memory for
   "activate this button") would do nothing.
2. No `aria-current="page"` on the active item - the active page was only
   distinguished visually (a CSS class), invisible to assistive tech.

## Fixes made

`desktop/src/App.css`:
- Added `--sev-info-text`/`--sev-error-text`, dedicated badge-text color
  variables per theme, rather than reusing `--text-muted`/`--error` (which
  pass contrast everywhere else they're used - changing them globally would
  risk regressing an already-passing area to fix a narrower one).
  Dark: `#b0ada5` (5.12:1) / `#dc7a70` (5.28:1). Light info: `#5a564f`
  (5.53:1). Light error unchanged (`#a3312a`, already 6.06:1).
- Added `.nav-item:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); ... }` -
  there was no custom focus style at all before this (relying on browser
  default, unverified whether visible enough against this dark sidebar).

`desktop/src/components/Sidebar.tsx`: `onKeyDown` now handles `" "` alongside
`"Enter"` (with `e.preventDefault()`, matching native button behaviour of
suppressing the page-scroll Space normally triggers); `aria-current="page"`
added to the active item.

`desktop/src/onboarding/Wizard.tsx`: root `<div className="wizard-overlay">`
changed to `<main className="wizard-overlay">` - semantically correct, since
it genuinely is the page's main content while shown, and required no other
DOM restructuring (React fragment/children unchanged).

## Round 2 - re-verified against the live app, not just re-read

Re-ran axe against all 8 pages (both themes) and all 6 wizard steps after
the fixes, same method: **0 violations everywhere.**

Real keyboard-operability check, done carefully because the first attempt
was misleading: this session's browser-automation tool's synthetic
`key`/`space`/`Return` presses did not trigger the focused element's React
handler in this environment (confirmed on a native, unmodified `<button>`
too - the sidebar language toggle - which also failed to respond to a
synthetic Return press even before this session touched anything, ruling out
a code regression and pointing at a tool/environment limitation instead of
a product defect). Dispatching a genuine `KeyboardEvent('keydown', {key:
' '})` directly at the focused nav item via `element.dispatchEvent(...)`
DID correctly trigger navigation and update `aria-current` - confirming the
fix's logic itself is correct, even though this session could not observe
it through a literal synthesized hardware keypress in this specific browser
harness. Documented honestly rather than either claiming a full live
keyboard test that didn't actually work, or silently skipping verification.

## Verification

- `npx tsc --noEmit` (desktop/): clean.
- `npm run build`: succeeds (300KB/87KB gzip JS, no regression in bundle
  size from these changes).
- axe-core 4.10.2 against all 8 pages × 2 color schemes + 6 wizard steps:
  0 violations post-fix (26+ real violation instances found and fixed
  pre-fix).
- Manual DOM inspection (`role`, `tabIndex`, `aria-current`, computed
  `outline` style) confirms the ARIA/focus-style fixes are actually present
  and computed correctly at runtime, not just present in source.
- Disposable `desktop/dist/` verification build output removed afterward.

## Scope and honest limitations

- This is one pass with one tool (axe-core) against the Demo-mode desktop
  shell + wizard. It is not a full manual screen-reader walkthrough (NVDA/
  VoiceOver), not a full keyboard-only end-to-end task completion test (the
  tool limitation above blocked a literal synthesized-keypress version of
  this - a human tester with a real keyboard should still do this once),
  and not a zoom/reduced-motion pass (`prefers-reduced-motion` is not
  referenced anywhere in `App.css` - there is very little animation in this
  UI to begin with, but this was not verified, only noted).
- `Diagnostics`/`Incidents`/`Timeline` were the only pages with any severity
  badges in the current demo data set at all - the contrast fix is a CSS
  rule fix (applies everywhere `.sev-info`/`.sev-error` render), not a
  per-page patch, so it is not data-set-dependent, but only those 3 pages'
  pre-fix violations were directly observed.
- No color-blindness simulation was run (axe's contrast checks are luminance-
  based, not a substitute for a deuteranopia/protanopia pass on the
  causes/correlates/precedes line-style distinction `App.css` already uses
  specifically BECAUSE color alone was deemed insufficient there - that
  design decision predates this audit and was not re-tested here).

Phase 3 is not fully closed by this one log - the "no formal audit" gap
specifically named in `PROGRESS.md` is now addressed; the keyboard/zoom/
reduced-motion items beyond basic nav focusability remain open, narrower
work.

## Second addendum - color-blindness simulation, the causes/correlates/precedes line distinction

`App.css`'s `.relation-causes`/`.relation-correlates`/`.relation-precedes`
rules (solid/dashed/dotted `border-top-style`, with `causes` also getting a
distinct `--warn-line` color while `correlates`/`precedes` share
`--neutral-line`) were designed, per the code's own framing, specifically
so this distinction does not rely on color alone. Verified rather than
taken on faith: injected real SVG `feColorMatrix` filters (deuteranopia,
protanopia, and full grayscale - the strictest case, removing all hue and
saturation information) via `document.documentElement.style.filter` on the
live Incidents page (which has real examples of all three relation types in
the current demo data) and inspected the result directly.

**Result: the distinction holds up completely, even in full grayscale** -
solid, dashed, and finely-dotted lines remain visually distinct by pattern
alone with zero color information present, and every relation line is also
paired with a plain-language label ("which caused", "and at the same time",
"and then, without a known link") that reinforces the same information
redundantly. This is a real, positive finding, not a gap: the earlier
design decision to avoid relying on color alone is now empirically
confirmed to actually work, not just intended to.

## Addendum - reduced-motion and zoom, checked directly rather than left open

**Reduced motion**: `grep -rn "transition|animation|@keyframes" desktop/src/`
found zero matches in any `.tsx`/`.css` file (the only hits were unrelated
JSON data fields named "transition" describing TCP state changes in demo
event fixtures, not CSS). There is no motion anywhere in this UI to gate
behind `prefers-reduced-motion` - adding an empty media query would be
boilerplate with no effect. Verified absence, not an unexamined gap.

**Zoom**: spot-checked at 200% browser zoom (`document.body.style.zoom =
'2'`) on Overview and Incidents (the page with the most complex layout -
severity badges, causal-chain connector lines) in Arabic/RTL. Text wraps
correctly, no interactive element became unreachable or overlapped another,
and a horizontal scrollbar appeared for content wider than the zoomed
viewport - acceptable under WCAG 1.4.10 (Reflow), which permits scrolling
in one dimension, not zero. Not a full pass (didn't check all 8 pages at
multiple zoom levels, and only tested programmatic `zoom` CSS rather than a
real browser Ctrl+/Ctrl- keyboard zoom, which this session's browser tool
does not support triggering), but a real, positive first data point rather
than an unexamined "not done."
